{
  "requirement": "Lists the selectable items from a single parse, including the root for the whole file.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Outline {\n    pub items: Vec<OutlineItem>,\n}\n\nimpl Outline {\n    pub fn new(items: Vec<OutlineItem>) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for Outline;\nimpl Debug for Outline;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.sdk.outlines.Outline` is a type contract. It has four parts:\n- `Outline` is `#[non_exhaustive]` and has one public field, `items: Vec<OutlineItem>`.\n- `Outline::new(items: Vec<OutlineItem>) -> Self` exists.\n- `Outline` implements `Clone`.\n- `Outline` implements `Debug`.\n\nThe prose sentence says an outline lists the selectable items from a single parse, including the root. That behavior is produced by the parsers and the outline-content code. Other requirements cover it, such as `requirements.sdk.outlines.items` and `requirements.sdk.outlines.selection`.\n\n**Test mapping.** The only test marked for this requirement is `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`. It builds rustdoc for the crate and calls `documented_exports`, which calls `check_declaration` for every exported item, including `Outline`. `check_declaration` reads the requirement's own text and compares it with rustdoc.\n- **Trait impls.** `rustdoc.trait_impls(page)` must equal the set of `impl ...` lines in the requirement's second code block, here exactly `impl Clone for Outline` and `impl Debug for Outline`. The assertion is set equality, so a missing or an extra trait impl fails.\n- **Members.** `link.members` must equal `rustdoc.member_names(page)`, which covers the fields and methods rustdoc lists for the type.\n- **Declaration and constructor.** `signature_parts` compares the requirement's declaration with rustdoc's published declaration. The comparison covers the struct declaration, including `#[non_exhaustive]` and the `pub items: Vec<OutlineItem>` field. It also covers each inherent member, here `pub fn new(items: Vec<OutlineItem>) -> Self`. Both differences are asserted empty, so a changed field type, a changed `new` signature, or an added or removed member fails.\n- **`#[non_exhaustive]`.** `check_declaration` also asserts that a published struct with public fields is non-exhaustive.\n- **Comparison logic.** `member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed` checks that `signature_parts` and `normalize` catch type and receiver changes. It is marked for `requirements.sdk.public_api`, not for this requirement.\n\n**Behavior.** Neither `new` nor the `Clone` and `Debug` impls has conditional logic of its own to test. `new` passes its `items` argument through unchanged. `Clone` and `Debug` are presumably derived, though I did not read the source. Behavioral use of `Outline::new` appears in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs`, for example in `CustomLanguage`'s `outline` and in `fn[unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors]`. Those tests assert on `outline().items` and carry their own `outlines.*` markings. They are not needed for this type-contract requirement.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. A violation such as dropping `non_exhaustive`, changing the field type, changing the constructor signature, or removing or adding a trait impl would make `public_exports_match_the_appendix` fail. No acknowledgments apply.",
